WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.900
Welcome to Chemical Thermodynamics.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.620
This recorded Chemistry class uses an AI generated Marin voice.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:11.420
Begin every energy calculation by choosing the system.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.600
An open system exchanges matter and energy with its surroundings.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.340
A closed system exchanges energy but not matter.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.140
An isolated system exchanges neither.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.308
Internal energy, enthalpy and entropy are state functions: their changes

00:00:27.308 --> 00:00:27.660
depend

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.320
on initial and final states.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.420
Heat and work describe energy transfer along a path.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:38.300
Temperature and pressure are intensive properties; total volume and total

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.740
internal energy are extensive.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.580
In the Chemistry convention, the first law is change in internal energy equals

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.500
heat plus work done on the system.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.240
Heat entering is positive.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.220
Compression work is positive, while expansion work is negative.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.900
For expansion against a constant external pressure, work is minus external

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.660
pressure times the volume increase.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.940
Consider a closed system that absorbs five hundred joules and expands by one

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.080
litre against two bar.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.840
One litre bar equals one hundred joules.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.100
The work is therefore minus two hundred joules, and the internal energy

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.860
increases by three hundred joules.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.320
The system gained heat but returned some energy to the surroundings as work.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.460
Notice that external pressure appears in this work expression.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.260
If it varies, the appropriate pressure volume integral is needed.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.520
Free expansion against zero external pressure does no pressure volume work.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.040
Adiabatic means no heat transfer; it does not automatically mean constant

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:34.460
temperature.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.900
Isothermal means constant temperature.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.260
Isochoric means constant volume, and isobaric means constant pressure.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.640
For an ideal gas, internal energy depends only on temperature, so an

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:46.240
isothermal

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.680
change has zero internal energy change even though heat and work may each be

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.100
nonzero.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.980
Keep the signs tied to the chosen system, and always convert litre bar into

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.300
energy units before adding heat and work.

00:01:58.656 --> 00:02:03.236
Enthalpy is defined as internal energy plus pressure times volume.

00:02:04.176 --> 00:02:08.036
For a closed system undergoing only pressure volume work at constant pressure,

00:02:08.576 --> 00:02:10.936
the heat transferred equals the enthalpy change.

00:02:11.476 --> 00:02:15.554
At constant volume, with no other work, heat equals the internal energy

00:02:15.554 --> 00:02:15.956
change.

00:02:16.476 --> 00:02:18.296
The conditions are part of the result.

00:02:19.156 --> 00:02:23.476
An exothermic process has a negative enthalpy change, while an endothermic

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:25.076
process has a positive one.

00:02:25.636 --> 00:02:29.956
For ideal gaseous reactants and products at the same temperature, the gas

00:02:29.956 --> 00:02:34.556
contribution gives change in enthalpy equals change in internal energy plus

00:02:34.556 --> 00:02:37.396
change in gaseous mole number times R T.

00:02:38.296 --> 00:02:42.556
In ordinary reaction calculations this form neglects condensed phase pressure

00:02:42.556 --> 00:02:43.576
volume contributions.

00:02:44.796 --> 00:02:47.976
Count only gaseous species when finding the gaseous mole change.

00:02:48.536 --> 00:02:53.244
For one nitrogen plus three hydrogen forming two ammonia, all gases, the

00:02:53.244 --> 00:02:53.636
change

00:02:53.636 --> 00:02:57.396
is two minus four, or minus two moles per reaction as written.

00:02:58.556 --> 00:03:02.776
Suppose the internal energy change is minus ninety point zero kilojoules for

00:03:02.776 --> 00:03:04.636
that reaction amount at three hundred kelvin.

00:03:05.656 --> 00:03:10.236
Use R equal to zero point zero zero eight three one four kilojoules per mole

00:03:10.236 --> 00:03:10.556
kelvin.

00:03:11.316 --> 00:03:15.877
The correction is minus two times R times three hundred, about minus four

00:03:15.877 --> 00:03:16.189
point

00:03:16.189 --> 00:03:17.116
nine nine kilojoules.

00:03:18.036 --> 00:03:21.576
The enthalpy change is about minus ninety five point zero kilojoules.

00:03:22.656 --> 00:03:26.456
Reversing the reaction reverses both energy changes and the gaseous mole

00:03:26.456 --> 00:03:26.916
change.

00:03:27.456 --> 00:03:32.216
A separate useful relation for one mole of an ideal gas is molar heat capacity

00:03:32.216 --> 00:03:37.356
at constant pressure minus molar heat capacity at constant volume equals R.

00:03:37.956 --> 00:03:41.996
It is not a universal identity for liquids, solids or real gases.

00:03:43.136 --> 00:03:47.306
Heat capacity relates heat to temperature change under specified conditions;

00:03:47.306 --> 00:03:47.416
if

00:03:47.416 --> 00:03:51.436
it varies appreciably with temperature, use an integral instead of a constant

00:03:51.436 --> 00:03:51.836
value.

00:03:52.876 --> 00:03:56.336
Check the phase labels and the reaction amount before substituting numbers.

00:03:56.856 --> 00:03:59.716
Hess's law follows from enthalpy being a state function.

00:04:00.396 --> 00:04:03.976
A reaction may be assembled from several steps; adding their enthalpy changes

00:04:03.976 --> 00:04:05.616
gives the same overall change.

00:04:06.396 --> 00:04:07.916
Reverse a step and reverse its sign.

00:04:08.756 --> 00:04:11.896
Multiply an equation by a number and multiply its enthalpy by that number.

00:04:12.716 --> 00:04:13.956
Here is an illustrative cycle.

00:04:14.856 --> 00:04:19.641
Carbon as graphite plus oxygen forming carbon dioxide has enthalpy minus three

00:04:19.641 --> 00:04:21.976
hundred ninety four kilojoules per mole of reaction.

00:04:22.856 --> 00:04:27.436
Carbon monoxide plus half an oxygen forming carbon dioxide has enthalpy minus

00:04:27.436 --> 00:04:29.036
two hundred eighty three.

00:04:29.036 --> 00:04:32.176
We want graphite plus half an oxygen forming carbon monoxide.

00:04:33.056 --> 00:04:35.256
Keep the first equation and reverse the second.

00:04:36.096 --> 00:04:38.676
Carbon dioxide cancels, leaving the target reaction.

00:04:39.536 --> 00:04:43.536
Add minus three hundred ninety four to plus two hundred eighty three to obtain

00:04:43.536 --> 00:04:45.296
minus one hundred eleven kilojoules.

00:04:45.976 --> 00:04:49.596
These rounded values illustrate the calculation; phases and reference

00:04:49.596 --> 00:04:50.676
conditions must match.

00:04:51.596 --> 00:04:55.156
Standard formation enthalpy refers to forming one mole of a substance from its

00:04:55.156 --> 00:04:56.396
elements in their reference states.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:05:00.236
The standard formation enthalpy of an element in its reference state is

00:05:00.236 --> 00:05:04.436
assigned zero; this does not say that its internal energy or entropy is zero.

00:05:05.436 --> 00:05:09.176
Multiply each formation enthalpy by its stoichiometric coefficient, then

00:05:09.176 --> 00:05:11.156
subtract the reactant sum from the product sum.

00:05:11.936 --> 00:05:15.576
Combustion enthalpy concerns complete burning of the specified amount, usually

00:05:15.576 --> 00:05:17.816
one mole, with product phases stated.

00:05:18.696 --> 00:05:20.236
Atomization creates gaseous atoms.

00:05:20.936 --> 00:05:22.576
Sublimation converts solid to gas.

00:05:23.176 --> 00:05:27.576
Ionization removes an electron from a gaseous species, while hydration brings

00:05:27.576 --> 00:05:28.816
gaseous ions into water.

00:05:29.656 --> 00:05:32.796
Dissolution can combine lattice separation and solvation effects.

00:05:33.476 --> 00:05:37.576
For a gas phase bond estimate, add energies of bonds broken and subtract

00:05:37.576 --> 00:05:38.856
energies of bonds formed.

00:05:39.776 --> 00:05:43.308
Average bond enthalpies give an approximation because bond environments

00:05:43.308 --> 00:05:43.656
differ.

00:05:44.496 --> 00:05:48.656
Do not mix an average gas bond estimate with a liquid phase reaction without

00:05:48.656 --> 00:05:49.876
accounting for phase changes.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.460
Energy conservation alone does not choose the direction of a process.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.340
Entropy supplies the second law criterion: a spontaneous change increases the

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.600
total entropy of the system and surroundings.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.880
The system's entropy may decrease if the surroundings gain more.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.920
A reversible ideal limit has zero total entropy production.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:16.780
For a reversible isothermal path, entropy change is reversible heat divided by

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:17.140
temperature.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:22.380
More generally, integrate reversible heat divided by temperature along a

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:23.640
suitable path between the states.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.140
Entropy is a state function, even when the actual path is irreversible.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.600
For the usual closed system at constant temperature and pressure, without

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:37.620
imposed non pressure volume work, a negative Gibbs energy change identifies a

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.300
thermodynamically favourable direction.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:44.060
Gibbs energy change is enthalpy change minus temperature times entropy change.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:45.900
Temperature must be in kelvin.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.640
Match the energy units before subtracting.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.560
Suppose enthalpy change is minus forty kilojoules and entropy change is minus

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:54.920
one hundred joules per kelvin.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.600
Convert the entropy to minus zero point one zero zero kilojoules per kelvin.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:04.660
At three hundred kelvin, Gibbs energy change is minus forty plus thirty, or

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:05.640
minus ten kilojoules.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.160
At five hundred kelvin, it is minus forty plus fifty, or plus ten.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.420
If both enthalpy and entropy changes are approximately temperature independent

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.560
over the range, the crossover is four hundred kelvin.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:22.120
This exothermic process with decreasing entropy is favoured at lower

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:22.540
temperature.

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.240
Positive enthalpy and positive entropy favour higher temperature.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.560
Negative enthalpy and positive entropy favour the forward direction throughout

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.320
the temperature range where those signs hold.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:34.220
The opposite pair does not.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.920
Finally, favourable does not mean fast.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:41.020
A large activation barrier can make a thermodynamically favourable reaction

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.000
very slow.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.220
Keep the direction question separate from the kinetics question.

00:07:45.432 --> 00:07:49.352
The standard Gibbs energy change and the Gibbs energy change for the current

00:07:49.352 --> 00:07:50.932
mixture are different quantities.

00:07:51.732 --> 00:07:56.752
The connection is Gibbs energy change equals standard Gibbs energy change plus

00:07:56.752 --> 00:08:00.812
R T times the natural logarithm of the reaction quotient Q.

00:08:01.732 --> 00:08:05.412
The rigorous quotient uses dimensionless activities relative to standard

00:08:05.412 --> 00:08:05.812
states.

00:08:06.872 --> 00:08:11.372
For ideal dilute solutions and ideal gases, concentration or pressure ratios

00:08:11.372 --> 00:08:13.272
provide useful approximations.

00:08:13.902 --> 00:08:19.032
At equilibrium, the reaction Gibbs energy change is zero and Q equals the

00:08:19.032 --> 00:08:20.332
equilibrium constant K.

00:08:21.432 --> 00:08:26.412
Substitution gives standard Gibbs energy change equals minus R T times the

00:08:26.412 --> 00:08:27.772
natural logarithm of K.

00:08:29.012 --> 00:08:33.016
Thus a negative standard Gibbs energy change implies K greater than one at

00:08:33.016 --> 00:08:33.232
that

00:08:33.232 --> 00:08:33.512
temperature.

00:08:34.232 --> 00:08:37.452
It does not guarantee that every possible mixture will proceed forward.

00:08:38.402 --> 00:08:42.687
Take standard Gibbs energy change approximately minus five point seven four

00:08:42.687 --> 00:08:45.452
four kilojoules per mole at three hundred kelvin.

00:08:46.732 --> 00:08:50.892
With R equal to zero point zero zero eight three one four kilojoules per mole

00:08:50.892 --> 00:08:56.032
kelvin, the natural logarithm of K is about two point three zero three.

00:08:56.032 --> 00:08:57.452
K is approximately ten.

00:08:58.332 --> 00:09:00.732
If Q is one, the forward direction is favoured.

00:09:00.732 --> 00:09:05.105
If Q is one hundred, Gibbs energy change is positive, about plus five point

00:09:05.105 --> 00:09:09.592
seven four kilojoules per mole, and the reverse direction is favoured.

00:09:09.592 --> 00:09:11.692
If Q is ten, the mixture is at equilibrium.

00:09:12.652 --> 00:09:17.412
Combining the equations gives Gibbs energy change equals R T times the natural

00:09:17.412 --> 00:09:19.292
logarithm of Q divided by K.

00:09:20.392 --> 00:09:22.512
This directly connects composition to direction.

00:09:23.712 --> 00:09:26.672
Standard conditions do not automatically mean two hundred ninety eight kelvin;

00:09:27.232 --> 00:09:28.592
always read the stated temperature.

00:09:29.332 --> 00:09:33.188
Finish with a short checklist: define the system, choose the correct work

00:09:33.188 --> 00:09:33.452
sign,

00:09:34.212 --> 00:09:37.792
preserve reaction coefficients and phases, convert entropy units, and

00:09:37.792 --> 00:09:39.012
distinguish Q from K.

00:09:39.852 --> 00:09:42.492
Use the worked notes and six original practice questions.

00:09:43.232 --> 00:09:47.172
This is the core Unit four foundation; detailed enthalpy cycles, entropy

00:09:47.172 --> 00:09:50.232
integrals and wider mixed problems remain for further study.

00:09:50.448 --> 00:09:54.648
Formation enthalpies let us assemble a reaction without measuring it directly.

00:09:55.888 --> 00:09:58.948
Use the balanced coefficients and the physical states in the data.

00:09:59.908 --> 00:10:03.988
For methane combustion to carbon dioxide gas and liquid water, take

00:10:03.988 --> 00:10:08.588
illustrative standard formation enthalpies in kilojoules per mole: methane

00:10:08.588 --> 00:10:13.348
minus seventy five, carbon dioxide minus three hundred ninety four, and liquid

00:10:13.348 --> 00:10:16.128
water minus two hundred eighty six.

00:10:16.128 --> 00:10:18.268
Oxygen in its reference state contributes zero.

00:10:18.988 --> 00:10:22.803
Products contribute minus three hundred ninety four plus twice minus two

00:10:22.803 --> 00:10:26.908
hundred eighty six, giving minus nine hundred sixty six.

00:10:26.908 --> 00:10:30.048
Subtract the reactant contribution of minus seventy five.

00:10:30.048 --> 00:10:33.928
The reaction enthalpy is minus eight hundred ninety one kilojoules per mole of

00:10:33.928 --> 00:10:35.288
methane for these rounded data.

00:10:36.368 --> 00:10:38.428
Changing the product to water vapour changes the answer.

00:10:39.348 --> 00:10:42.688
Average bond enthalpies instead estimate energy for gas phase bonds broken

00:10:42.688 --> 00:10:43.788
minus bonds formed.

00:10:43.788 --> 00:10:48.048
They are approximate and do not automatically include condensation or solid

00:10:48.048 --> 00:10:49.148
lattice effects.

00:10:49.448 --> 00:10:53.148
An element's zero standard formation enthalpy applies to its reference state,

00:10:53.628 --> 00:10:56.128
not to every allotrope, gaseous atom or ion.

00:10:56.848 --> 00:10:59.848
Always label the amount and phase beside a thermochemical result.

00:11:00.096 --> 00:11:05.076
An ionic dissolution cycle separates processes that are easily confused.

00:11:06.096 --> 00:11:09.676
First imagine pulling the solid into separated gaseous ions.

00:11:10.676 --> 00:11:13.516
Define this lattice dissociation enthalpy as positive.

00:11:14.236 --> 00:11:18.276
Then hydrate those gaseous ions to the chosen aqueous reference states;

00:11:19.116 --> 00:11:21.296
hydration enthalpies are normally negative.

00:11:22.256 --> 00:11:26.436
Adding both stages gives the enthalpy of solution for matching endpoints and

00:11:26.436 --> 00:11:27.416
concentration conventions.

00:11:27.416 --> 00:11:32.296
With illustrative values of plus seven hundred eighty kilojoules per mole for

00:11:32.296 --> 00:11:36.556
lattice dissociation and minus seven hundred fifty for total hydration,

00:11:37.416 --> 00:11:40.436
dissolution has enthalpy plus thirty kilojoules per mole.

00:11:41.216 --> 00:11:45.316
A positive result does not by itself prove that dissolution is impossible,

00:11:46.036 --> 00:11:49.296
because the Gibbs energy also contains an entropy contribution.

00:11:50.296 --> 00:11:54.996
Some tables instead define lattice enthalpy for forming the solid from gaseous

00:11:54.996 --> 00:11:55.396
ions.

00:11:55.836 --> 00:11:59.096
That is the opposite process and has the opposite sign.

00:12:00.056 --> 00:12:02.636
Never insert a number until its process is clear.

00:12:03.576 --> 00:12:08.395
Atomization, ionization and electron attachment provide further steps in a

00:12:08.395 --> 00:12:08.656
full

00:12:08.656 --> 00:12:09.496
Born Haber cycle.

00:12:10.456 --> 00:12:12.896
Keep each species and charge balanced when adding them.

00:12:13.596 --> 00:12:18.234
This two stage example isolates dissolution bookkeeping rather than claiming

00:12:18.234 --> 00:12:18.356
to

00:12:18.356 --> 00:12:21.096
reproduce every step of a complete formation cycle.

00:12:21.648 --> 00:12:25.202
A calorimeter records a temperature change in material surrounding the

00:12:25.202 --> 00:12:25.608
reacting

00:12:25.608 --> 00:12:25.988
system.

00:12:27.088 --> 00:12:29.308
Include every heat capacity specified in the problem.

00:12:30.388 --> 00:12:34.503
Suppose one hundred grams of solution has specific heat capacity four point

00:12:34.503 --> 00:12:34.668
two

00:12:34.668 --> 00:12:38.768
joules per gram kelvin, and the calorimeter contributes eighty joules per

00:12:38.768 --> 00:12:38.908
kelvin.

00:12:39.988 --> 00:12:43.888
The combined heat capacity is four hundred twenty plus eighty, or five hundred

00:12:43.888 --> 00:12:44.688
joules per kelvin.

00:12:45.228 --> 00:12:48.768
A temperature rise of two kelvin means the measured surroundings gain one

00:12:48.768 --> 00:12:49.388
thousand joules.

00:12:50.228 --> 00:12:54.688
If heat loss is negligible, the reaction releases one thousand joules.

00:12:54.868 --> 00:12:59.042
For zero point zero five zero mole reacting as written, the molar heat is

00:12:59.042 --> 00:12:59.328
minus

00:12:59.328 --> 00:13:00.488
twenty kilojoules per mole.

00:13:01.548 --> 00:13:04.834
The negative sign belongs to the reaction, while the solution and vessel

00:13:04.834 --> 00:13:05.108
gained

00:13:05.108 --> 00:13:05.388
heat.

00:13:06.168 --> 00:13:09.471
At approximately constant pressure with only pressure volume work, the

00:13:09.471 --> 00:13:09.848
reaction

00:13:09.848 --> 00:13:11.868
heat corresponds to enthalpy change.

00:13:12.668 --> 00:13:16.308
In a rigid bomb calorimeter, the corresponding constant volume heat gives

00:13:16.308 --> 00:13:20.868
internal energy change; convert to enthalpy when the gas mole correction is

00:13:20.868 --> 00:13:21.228
required.

00:13:22.228 --> 00:13:25.368
Check whether a supplied calorimeter constant already includes the solution

00:13:25.368 --> 00:13:27.348
before adding its heat capacity again.

00:13:28.008 --> 00:13:32.828
Entropy change must be evaluated using a reversible reference path between the

00:13:32.828 --> 00:13:33.648
same endpoints.

00:13:34.608 --> 00:13:38.808
Heat divided by temperature is not a universal expression for an arbitrary

00:13:38.808 --> 00:13:39.908
irreversible transfer.

00:13:40.868 --> 00:13:45.588
At an equilibrium phase transition at constant temperature and pressure, the

00:13:45.588 --> 00:13:49.808
molar entropy change is the molar transition enthalpy divided by that

00:13:49.808 --> 00:13:50.108
temperature.

00:13:50.988 --> 00:13:55.157
For melting with illustrative enthalpy six kilojoules per mole at three

00:13:55.157 --> 00:13:55.568
hundred

00:13:55.568 --> 00:13:59.428
kelvin, the entropy increase is twenty joules per mole kelvin.

00:14:00.848 --> 00:14:04.748
Freezing between the reverse endpoints gives minus twenty.

00:14:04.748 --> 00:14:08.528
For warming a substance at constant pressure with approximately constant molar

00:14:08.528 --> 00:14:11.888
heat capacity, integrate heat capacity divided by temperature.

00:14:12.708 --> 00:14:17.028
The result is molar heat capacity times the natural logarithm of final

00:14:17.028 --> 00:14:18.988
temperature divided by initial temperature.

00:14:19.968 --> 00:14:23.978
With thirty joules per mole kelvin and warming from three hundred to six

00:14:23.978 --> 00:14:27.928
hundred kelvin, the increase is thirty times the natural logarithm of two,

00:14:28.408 --> 00:14:30.528
about twenty point eight joules per mole kelvin.

00:14:31.508 --> 00:14:33.328
Both temperatures must be absolute.

00:14:34.128 --> 00:14:38.668
If heat capacity varies appreciably, retain it inside the integral rather than

00:14:38.668 --> 00:14:40.388
reusing the constant value shortcut.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:45.820
A system can lose entropy while a spontaneous process increases the total

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:47.580
entropy of system and surroundings.

00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:52.480
Consider an illustrative process at three hundred kelvin and constant pressure

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:56.480
that releases six kilojoules per mole into a large thermal reservoir.

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:00.920
Assume only pressure volume work and negligible changes elsewhere.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:06.240
The reservoir receives six thousand joules per mole, so its entropy increases

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:08.040
by twenty joules per mole kelvin.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:13.100
If the system entropy decreases by ten joules per mole kelvin, the total

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:14.220
increases by ten.

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:18.800
The process is thermodynamically favourable under those stated conditions.

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:23.839
Its Gibbs energy change is minus six kilojoules minus three hundred times

00:15:23.839 --> 00:15:24.120
minus

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:28.580
zero point zero one zero kilojoules per kelvin, giving minus three kilojoules

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:29.080
per mole.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:33.820
Negative temperature times the universe entropy change gives the same result

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:34.900
under these constraints.

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:39.760
The reservoir temperature is effectively constant; do not divide a large

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:42.560
varying temperature heat transfer by one arbitrary temperature.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:47.060
This example also shows why exothermic and spontaneous are different labels.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:52.240
Identify the surroundings, constraints and entropy contribution before using

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:54.160
the sign of heat as a direction test.

00:15:54.384 --> 00:15:59.724
Work depends on the path even when an ideal gas begins and ends at the same

00:15:59.724 --> 00:16:01.844
temperature and volumes.

00:16:01.844 --> 00:16:07.084
Consider one mole at three hundred kelvin expanding from two to four litres.

00:16:07.084 --> 00:16:11.984
For a reversible isothermal path, work done on the gas in the Chemistry sign

00:16:11.984 --> 00:16:16.464
convention is minus n R T times the natural logarithm of two.

00:16:17.464 --> 00:16:22.406
Using R eight point three one four joules per mole kelvin gives about minus

00:16:22.406 --> 00:16:22.604
one

00:16:22.604 --> 00:16:24.384
point seven two nine kilojoules.

00:16:25.524 --> 00:16:30.045
Now compare an isothermal expansion against constant external pressure equal

00:16:30.045 --> 00:16:30.164
to

00:16:30.164 --> 00:16:31.544
the final ideal gas pressure.

00:16:32.364 --> 00:16:35.924
The final pressure is n R T divided by the final volume.

00:16:36.804 --> 00:16:40.644
Multiplying that pressure by the two litre volume increase gives work about

00:16:40.644 --> 00:16:42.824
minus one point two four seven kilojoules.

00:16:43.324 --> 00:16:45.864
Use compatible volume units in both factors.

00:16:46.804 --> 00:16:50.624
The reversible path supplies a greater magnitude of expansion work between

00:16:50.624 --> 00:16:51.364
these endpoints.

00:16:52.304 --> 00:16:57.724
For the ideal gas, internal energy change is zero in both isothermal cases, so

00:16:57.724 --> 00:16:59.584
heat entering balances the negative work.

00:17:00.604 --> 00:17:04.504
Reversible does not mean zero work, and isothermal does not mean zero heat.

00:17:04.904 --> 00:17:09.584
The comparison assumes only pressure volume work, the stated ideal gas model

00:17:09.944 --> 00:17:12.224
and suitable heat exchange to maintain temperature.

00:17:12.552 --> 00:17:16.552
Adiabatic means no heat transfer, not unchanged temperature.

00:17:17.392 --> 00:17:21.632
As a supporting ideal gas application, take one mole of a monatomic gas

00:17:21.632 --> 00:17:23.072
initially at three hundred kelvin.

00:17:24.332 --> 00:17:28.292
Compress it reversibly and adiabatically to half its initial volume, using

00:17:28.292 --> 00:17:32.512
constant molar heat capacities and gamma equal to five thirds.

00:17:32.512 --> 00:17:36.662
The reversible ideal gas relation gives final temperature equal to three

00:17:36.662 --> 00:17:40.505
hundred times two raised to the two thirds power, or about four hundred

00:17:40.505 --> 00:17:40.884
seventy

00:17:40.884 --> 00:17:42.112
six point two kelvin.

00:17:43.392 --> 00:17:46.792
Internal energy rises by n C V times the temperature increase.

00:17:47.612 --> 00:17:51.992
With C V equal to three halves R, the rise is about two point one nine eight

00:17:51.992 --> 00:17:55.892
kilojoules using R eight point three one four.

00:17:55.892 --> 00:17:59.592
Heat is zero, so work done on the gas is positive by that amount in the

00:17:59.592 --> 00:18:00.352
Chemistry convention.

00:18:01.212 --> 00:18:04.952
An irreversible adiabatic change still has zero heat, but it cannot

00:18:04.952 --> 00:18:07.972
automatically use the same reversible volume temperature relation.

00:18:08.832 --> 00:18:12.612
Likewise a gas with temperature dependent heat capacities needs a more careful

00:18:12.612 --> 00:18:13.212
calculation.

00:18:14.312 --> 00:18:18.312
State reversibility, ideal behaviour and constant heat capacities before

00:18:18.312 --> 00:18:19.352
applying this power law.

00:18:20.272 --> 00:18:24.192
The example reinforces first law signs rather than replacing the separate

00:18:24.192 --> 00:18:26.092
Physics treatment of complete gas cycles.

00:18:26.352 --> 00:18:30.612
A reaction enthalpy quoted at one temperature need not have exactly the same

00:18:30.612 --> 00:18:32.052
value at another.

00:18:32.472 --> 00:18:36.132
A Hess cycle can connect the temperatures by warming reactants and products

00:18:36.132 --> 00:18:37.872
through their respective heat capacities.

00:18:38.712 --> 00:18:43.312
The reaction heat capacity change is the sum of product heat capacities with

00:18:43.312 --> 00:18:46.992
stoichiometric coefficients minus the corresponding reactant sum.

00:18:47.672 --> 00:18:51.792
If that difference is approximately constant over the range and no phase

00:18:51.792 --> 00:18:56.130
transition intervenes, the enthalpy correction is reaction heat capacity

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:56.492
change

00:18:56.492 --> 00:18:58.512
times the temperature increase.

00:18:59.212 --> 00:19:03.657
Take a supplied reaction enthalpy minus one hundred kilojoules per mole at two

00:19:03.657 --> 00:19:07.714
hundred ninety eight kelvin and reaction heat capacity change plus fifty

00:19:07.714 --> 00:19:08.052
joules

00:19:08.052 --> 00:19:08.852
per mole kelvin.

00:19:09.972 --> 00:19:13.392
Raising the temperature to three hundred ninety eight kelvin adds plus five

00:19:13.392 --> 00:19:18.532
kilojoules per mole, so the corrected reaction enthalpy is minus ninety five.

00:19:18.532 --> 00:19:22.772
The reaction remains exothermic in this model but is less exothermic at the

00:19:22.772 --> 00:19:23.392
higher temperature.

00:19:24.552 --> 00:19:26.292
Convert joules into kilojoules before adding.

00:19:26.852 --> 00:19:30.772
If heat capacity varies, keep it inside the temperature integral; if a phase

00:19:30.772 --> 00:19:33.192
transition occurs, include its enthalpy separately.

00:19:34.092 --> 00:19:38.352
This is a supporting temperature correction derived from state functions, not

00:19:38.352 --> 00:19:41.212
permission to extrapolate a constant heat capacity indefinitely.

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:45.840
Standard molar entropy values are combined with the balanced reaction

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:49.040
coefficients, not simply compared one species at a time.

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:54.180
Use the written reaction one nitrogen molecule plus three hydrogen molecules

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:57.220
giving two ammonia molecules, all gaseous.

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:02.138
Suppose supplied standard molar entropies are one hundred ninety two, one

00:20:02.138 --> 00:20:05.140
hundred thirty one and one hundred ninety three joules per mole kelvin

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:12.100
for

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:12.466
nitrogen, hydrogen and ammonia respectively.

00:20:12.466 --> 00:20:13.748
The product sum is two times one hundred ninety three, or three hundred eighty

00:20:13.748 --> 00:20:13.840
six.

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:17.725
The reactant sum is one hundred ninety two plus three times one hundred thirty

00:20:17.725 --> 00:20:20.460
one, or five hundred eighty five.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:23.900
Reaction entropy is therefore minus one hundred ninety nine joules per mole of

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:24.540
reaction kelvin.

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:29.480
All individual entropy values are positive while their reaction difference is

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:29.820
negative.

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:34.820
If the supplied reaction enthalpy is minus ninety two kilojoules per mole of

00:20:34.820 --> 00:20:39.160
reaction at two hundred ninety eight kelvin, standard Gibbs energy is about

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:42.980
minus thirty two point seven kilojoules after converting the entropy units.

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:46.120
The coefficients define the reaction amount throughout.

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:51.519
This calculation uses the stated standard states and supplied rounded data,

00:20:51.519 --> 00:20:51.700
not

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:55.320
a claim that every entropy can be inferred solely from counting gas molecules.

00:20:56.376 --> 00:21:00.616
A constant volume heat measurement and a reaction enthalpy are not

00:21:00.616 --> 00:21:01.756
automatically the same number.

00:21:02.396 --> 00:21:06.856
For a closed reaction system with only pressure volume work, constant volume

00:21:06.856 --> 00:21:08.696
heat gives internal energy change.

00:21:09.316 --> 00:21:13.456
At constant pressure the corresponding heat gives enthalpy change under the

00:21:13.456 --> 00:21:14.296
usual conditions.

00:21:15.656 --> 00:21:19.476
Consider the supplied illustrative reaction data for two hydrogen molecules

00:21:19.476 --> 00:21:24.436
plus one oxygen molecule forming two liquid water molecules at three hundred

00:21:24.436 --> 00:21:24.676
kelvin.

00:21:25.316 --> 00:21:29.416
Let internal energy change be minus five hundred sixty kilojoules per mole of

00:21:29.416 --> 00:21:30.196
that written reaction.

00:21:31.116 --> 00:21:35.336
Only gaseous species enter the ideal gas mole correction: there are zero

00:21:35.336 --> 00:21:39.516
gaseous product moles and three gaseous reactant moles, so the change is minus

00:21:39.516 --> 00:21:39.796
three.

00:21:40.576 --> 00:21:45.516
Using R zero point zero zero eight three one four kilojoules per mole kelvin,

00:21:46.116 --> 00:21:50.559
the enthalpy is minus five hundred sixty minus seven point four eight three,

00:21:50.559 --> 00:21:50.676
or

00:21:50.676 --> 00:21:54.216
about minus five hundred sixty seven point four eight kilojoules per mole of

00:21:54.216 --> 00:21:54.496
reaction.

00:21:55.776 --> 00:21:59.849
Producing one mole of liquid water corresponds to half the written reaction,

00:21:59.849 --> 00:21:59.956
so

00:21:59.956 --> 00:22:02.556
both energy changes must be halved for that basis.

00:22:03.536 --> 00:22:06.696
Including liquid water in the gas count would give the wrong correction.

00:22:07.796 --> 00:22:12.336
Preserve phases, coefficients and the stated reaction basis before comparing

00:22:12.336 --> 00:22:12.996
heat measurements.

00:22:13.344 --> 00:22:17.044
A reaction enthalpy belongs to a particular balanced equation.

00:22:17.844 --> 00:22:21.724
Consider the supplied equation two moles of hydrogen plus one mole of oxygen

00:22:22.024 --> 00:22:26.024
forming two moles of liquid water, with reaction enthalpy minus five hundred

00:22:26.024 --> 00:22:28.324
seventy two kilojoules per mole of reaction extent.

00:22:29.664 --> 00:22:33.114
Start with zero point three zero zero mole hydrogen and zero point one zero

00:22:33.114 --> 00:22:34.184
zero mole oxygen.

00:22:35.244 --> 00:22:38.704
Dividing each amount by its stoichiometric coefficient gives possible extents

00:22:38.704 --> 00:22:42.124
zero point one five zero and zero point one zero zero mole.

00:22:43.004 --> 00:22:45.784
Oxygen limits the extent to zero point one zero zero mole.

00:22:46.864 --> 00:22:50.654
The reaction forms zero point two zero zero mole water and leaves zero point

00:22:50.654 --> 00:22:52.044
one zero zero mole hydrogen.

00:22:52.864 --> 00:22:56.645
At constant pressure with only pressure volume work, the reaction heat is

00:22:56.645 --> 00:22:56.904
minus

00:22:56.904 --> 00:22:58.484
fifty seven point two kilojoules.

00:22:59.564 --> 00:23:03.084
Writing the equation for one mole water halves its reaction enthalpy but

00:23:03.084 --> 00:23:05.524
doubles the numerical extent for the same physical conversion.

00:23:06.164 --> 00:23:07.484
Their product stays unchanged.

00:23:08.544 --> 00:23:11.724
Distinguish reaction extent from product amount, and keep the written

00:23:11.724 --> 00:23:14.624
coefficient convention attached to every molar reaction enthalpy.

00:23:14.952 --> 00:23:19.067
At a phase transition carried out reversibly at its equilibrium temperature

00:23:19.067 --> 00:23:19.232
and

00:23:19.232 --> 00:23:22.983
pressure, the entropy change is the transition enthalpy divided by the

00:23:22.983 --> 00:23:23.412
absolute

00:23:23.412 --> 00:23:24.372
transition temperature.

00:23:25.352 --> 00:23:29.572
Use supplied illustrative fusion enthalpy six point zero zero kilojoules per

00:23:29.572 --> 00:23:31.152
mole at three hundred kelvin.

00:23:32.392 --> 00:23:36.372
Melting two point zero zero moles absorbs twelve point zero kilojoules and

00:23:36.372 --> 00:23:39.832
raises the system entropy by forty point zero joules per kelvin.

00:23:39.832 --> 00:23:44.772
A thermal reservoir at the same temperature loses forty point zero joules per

00:23:44.772 --> 00:23:46.392
kelvin in the reversible limit.

00:23:47.212 --> 00:23:50.732
The total is zero, consistent with equilibrium rather than a finite

00:23:50.732 --> 00:23:52.152
thermodynamic driving force.

00:23:53.092 --> 00:23:55.292
Freezing reverses both signs for the same endpoints.

00:23:56.112 --> 00:23:59.592
This shortcut uses the transition temperature, not an arbitrary room

00:23:59.592 --> 00:23:59.972
temperature.

00:24:00.912 --> 00:24:05.132
Away from phase equilibrium, use an appropriate reversible reference path with

00:24:05.132 --> 00:24:06.912
heating or cooling stages as needed.

00:24:07.672 --> 00:24:11.952
A positive entropy change of the sample alone does not establish spontaneity,

00:24:12.492 --> 00:24:14.392
because the surroundings must also be included.

00:24:15.392 --> 00:24:19.332
State the phase, amount, pressure and transition temperature before applying

00:24:19.332 --> 00:24:19.832
the ratio.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:24.860
Heat transferred across a finite temperature difference produces entropy even

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:26.140
when energy is conserved.

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:31.220
Suppose an isolated combined model contains ideal reservoirs at four hundred

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:32.320
and three hundred kelvin.

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:37.083
One thousand joules flows from the hot reservoir to the cold reservoir, with

00:24:37.083 --> 00:24:37.180
no

00:24:37.180 --> 00:24:39.020
work and no energy loss elsewhere.

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:43.560
The hot reservoir loses two point five zero joules per kelvin of entropy.

00:24:44.420 --> 00:24:47.520
The cold reservoir gains three point three three joules per kelvin.

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:52.300
Their total entropy change is positive zero point eight three three joule per

00:24:52.300 --> 00:24:52.540
kelvin.

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:57.720
The two energy changes cancel, but the entropy changes do not, because equal

00:24:57.720 --> 00:24:59.420
heat is divided by different temperatures.

00:25:00.680 --> 00:25:03.880
Reversing only this heat transfer would make the combined entropy change

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:07.320
negative and cannot happen spontaneously in the isolated model.

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:12.280
A refrigerator can move heat in that direction only with additional processes

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:13.640
and an energy supply.

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:18.680
The reservoir approximation means each temperature stays essentially constant

00:25:18.680 --> 00:25:19.440
during the transfer.

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:24.500
Finite bodies that noticeably change temperature require integration rather

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:26.240
than using these two fixed denominators.

00:25:26.616 --> 00:25:31.316
Standard reaction Gibbs energy describes standard states, while actual

00:25:31.316 --> 00:25:33.196
direction also depends on composition.

00:25:34.276 --> 00:25:38.976
Use the relation reaction Gibbs energy equals R T times the natural logarithm

00:25:38.976 --> 00:25:40.596
of Q divided by K.

00:25:41.416 --> 00:25:45.116
For an illustrative reaction at three hundred kelvin, let the dimensionless

00:25:45.116 --> 00:25:49.438
equilibrium constant be ten and the current dimensionless reaction quotient

00:25:49.438 --> 00:25:49.611
one

00:25:49.611 --> 00:25:50.186
hundred.

00:25:50.186 --> 00:25:52.736
The quotient exceeds the equilibrium constant.

00:25:53.596 --> 00:25:57.476
Using R equal to eight point three one four joules per mole kelvin gives

00:25:57.476 --> 00:26:01.916
reaction Gibbs energy plus five point seven four kilojoules per mole of

00:26:01.916 --> 00:26:02.316
reaction.

00:26:03.416 --> 00:26:07.616
The forward reaction is thermodynamically uphill at this composition, so the

00:26:07.616 --> 00:26:09.376
reverse direction is favoured.

00:26:09.376 --> 00:26:14.116
Yet the standard reaction Gibbs energy is minus R T logarithm ten, or minus

00:26:14.116 --> 00:26:15.976
five point seven four kilojoules per mole.

00:26:16.576 --> 00:26:20.276
There is no contradiction: the two values refer to different conditions.

00:26:21.216 --> 00:26:24.656
At Q equal to K, actual reaction Gibbs energy is zero.

00:26:25.736 --> 00:26:29.636
Activities are dimensionless; concentration ratios approximate them only under

00:26:29.636 --> 00:26:30.456
suitable assumptions.

00:26:31.456 --> 00:26:34.636
Thermodynamic preference does not specify how quickly equilibrium is

00:26:34.636 --> 00:26:34.896
approached.

00:26:36.432 --> 00:26:41.132
A Born Haber cycle makes every intermediate in an ionic formation calculation

00:26:41.132 --> 00:26:41.752
explicit.

00:26:42.752 --> 00:26:46.814
Start with one mole of solid sodium and half a mole of gaseous chlorine,

00:26:46.814 --> 00:26:47.152
ending

00:26:47.152 --> 00:26:49.312
with one mole of solid sodium chloride.

00:26:50.112 --> 00:26:54.537
In a supplied teaching dataset, sodium sublimation costs one hundred eight

00:26:54.537 --> 00:26:55.652
kilojoules per mole.

00:26:56.052 --> 00:26:59.312
Its first ionization costs four hundred ninety six.

00:26:59.312 --> 00:27:04.152
Half the chlorine bond dissociation costs one hundred twenty two, and electron

00:27:04.152 --> 00:27:08.012
gain by gaseous chlorine releases three hundred forty nine.

00:27:08.012 --> 00:27:11.312
These signed stages total positive three hundred seventy seven kilojoules

00:27:11.312 --> 00:27:12.912
before lattice formation.

00:27:13.912 --> 00:27:17.712
If the standard formation enthalpy is negative four hundred eleven, gaseous

00:27:17.712 --> 00:27:21.392
ions must release seven hundred eighty eight kilojoules on forming the solid.

00:27:22.612 --> 00:27:25.998
Reversing that last arrow gives positive seven hundred eighty eight for

00:27:25.998 --> 00:27:26.332
lattice

00:27:26.332 --> 00:27:27.052
dissociation.

00:27:27.572 --> 00:27:31.912
The chlorine bond term is halved because the formation equation uses half a

00:27:31.912 --> 00:27:33.652
molecule of chlorine per formula unit.

00:27:34.632 --> 00:27:38.332
Electron gain is entered as a signed enthalpy change here, not as an unsigned

00:27:38.332 --> 00:27:39.252
affinity magnitude.

00:27:40.352 --> 00:27:42.792
State the arrow and convention before substituting any value.

00:27:43.488 --> 00:27:47.848
A constant heat capacity correction is a useful approximation, but a supplied

00:27:47.848 --> 00:27:49.948
temperature dependent function must be integrated.

00:27:50.828 --> 00:27:53.268
Keep the balanced reaction and its amount basis fixed.

00:27:54.168 --> 00:27:58.488
Suppose its product minus reactant heat capacity is ten plus zero point zero

00:27:58.488 --> 00:28:02.128
two zero times temperature, in joules per mole of reaction per kelvin.

00:28:02.848 --> 00:28:06.228
Take the standard reaction enthalpy at three hundred kelvin as negative fifty

00:28:06.228 --> 00:28:07.628
point zero kilojoules per mole.

00:28:07.628 --> 00:28:11.848
Between three hundred and five hundred kelvin, the constant term contributes

00:28:11.848 --> 00:28:13.208
two thousand joules per mole.

00:28:14.088 --> 00:28:18.008
The linear term integrates to zero point zero one zero times the difference of

00:28:18.008 --> 00:28:21.528
the squared temperatures, adding one thousand six hundred joules per mole.

00:28:22.808 --> 00:28:26.968
The total correction is positive three point six zero kilojoules, making the

00:28:26.968 --> 00:28:30.948
new enthalpy negative forty six point four zero.

00:28:30.948 --> 00:28:34.648
Do not insert one endpoint heat capacity into the constant formula and call it

00:28:34.648 --> 00:28:35.448
an exact integral.

00:28:35.988 --> 00:28:39.708
This supplied linear model is valid only over its stated interval with

00:28:39.708 --> 00:28:40.488
unchanged phases.

00:28:41.208 --> 00:28:44.788
A phase transition or different reaction normalization requires additional

00:28:44.788 --> 00:28:47.708
accounting rather than silently extending the same polynomial.

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:52.800
Consider one mole each of two chemically different ideal gases, initially

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:54.760
separated at the same temperature and pressure.

00:28:55.580 --> 00:28:57.040
Their starting volumes are equal.

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:01.520
Removing the partition lets each component spread into twice its initial

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:01.860
volume.

00:29:02.840 --> 00:29:06.700
A reversible reference expansion for each gas gives an entropy increase of the

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:09.000
gas constant times the natural logarithm of two.

00:29:09.980 --> 00:29:13.720
Adding the contributions gives about eleven point five three joules per kelvin

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:15.100
for the two mole sample.

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:19.360
This is a total entropy change, not a value per mole of mixture.

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:24.260
At three hundred kelvin the corresponding ideal mixing Gibbs change is about

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:28.220
negative three point four six kilojoules, since ideal mixing at fixed

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:30.140
temperature has zero enthalpy change.

00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:35.560
The spontaneous partition removal itself need not be reversible; entropy is

00:29:35.560 --> 00:29:36.980
evaluated through a reference path.

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:41.580
If both compartments instead contain the same gas at identical temperature and

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:46.000
pressure, removing their artificial partition creates no thermodynamic mixing

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:46.400
entropy.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:51.480
Do not label identical particles as different chemical components simply to

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:52.340
force a positive answer.

00:29:53.340 --> 00:29:57.060
Real gas interactions or unequal initial conditions require a different

00:29:57.060 --> 00:29:57.540
calculation.

00:29:58.008 --> 00:30:01.788
An equilibrium constant belongs to a written reaction at a specified

00:30:01.788 --> 00:30:02.288
temperature.

00:30:03.428 --> 00:30:06.828
Standard reaction Gibbs energy is also tied to that stoichiometric

00:30:06.828 --> 00:30:07.588
normalization.

00:30:08.828 --> 00:30:12.448
Suppose an original reaction has dimensionless equilibrium constant nine.

00:30:13.508 --> 00:30:17.368
Doubling every coefficient doubles its standard reaction Gibbs energy, so the

00:30:17.368 --> 00:30:21.148
new constant is eighty one, not eighteen.

00:30:21.148 --> 00:30:24.268
Reversing the original reaction changes the sign of its standard Gibbs energy

00:30:24.268 --> 00:30:27.048
and gives one ninth for its equilibrium constant.

00:30:28.328 --> 00:30:31.608
Halving the original coefficients instead gives the square root, which is

00:30:31.608 --> 00:30:31.868
three.

00:30:33.068 --> 00:30:37.866
These operations follow from standard reaction Gibbs energy equals negative R

00:30:37.866 --> 00:30:37.928
T

00:30:37.928 --> 00:30:39.668
times the natural logarithm of K.

00:30:40.468 --> 00:30:44.168
Multiplying an energy therefore multiplies the logarithm, rather than the

00:30:44.168 --> 00:30:44.668
constant itself.

00:30:45.428 --> 00:30:49.728
This bookkeeping does not change the physical equilibrium mixture: the new

00:30:49.728 --> 00:30:53.348
quotient is transformed by the same power, and it still equals the matching

00:30:53.348 --> 00:30:54.468
constant at equilibrium.

00:30:55.388 --> 00:30:58.928
Keep temperature fixed throughout the comparison and use dimensionless

00:30:58.928 --> 00:31:00.128
thermodynamic activities.

00:31:01.548 --> 00:31:04.908
Merely changing the initial amounts without changing the written reaction does

00:31:04.908 --> 00:31:06.108
not rescale its constant.

00:31:07.228 --> 00:31:11.128
Distinguish equation normalization from changing temperature or from moving a

00:31:11.128 --> 00:31:12.068
mixture away from equilibrium.

00:31:12.408 --> 00:31:16.808
Zero heat transfer does not force zero entropy change in an irreversible

00:31:16.808 --> 00:31:17.348
process.

00:31:18.328 --> 00:31:22.148
Consider one mole of an ideal gas initially confined to half of a rigid

00:31:22.148 --> 00:31:25.048
insulated container, with vacuum in the other half.

00:31:26.188 --> 00:31:29.268
Removing the internal partition doubles the volume available to the gas.

00:31:30.208 --> 00:31:34.231
External pressure opposing this free expansion is zero, so pressure volume

00:31:34.231 --> 00:31:34.448
work

00:31:34.448 --> 00:31:35.068
is zero.

00:31:35.748 --> 00:31:38.108
The insulated outer walls give zero heat transfer.

00:31:38.488 --> 00:31:42.728
In the Chemistry convention, internal energy change equals heat plus work and

00:31:42.728 --> 00:31:43.568
is therefore zero.

00:31:44.328 --> 00:31:48.668
For an ideal gas, internal energy depends only on temperature, so the final

00:31:48.668 --> 00:31:50.768
equilibrium temperature equals the initial one.

00:31:51.548 --> 00:31:52.968
Nevertheless entropy rises.

00:31:54.028 --> 00:31:57.448
Evaluate it through a reversible isothermal reference path between those same

00:31:57.448 --> 00:32:02.068
endpoints, giving R times the natural logarithm of two, approximately five

00:32:02.068 --> 00:32:03.668
point seven six joules per kelvin.

00:32:04.488 --> 00:32:08.448
This reference path calculates a state function; it is not the actual gas

00:32:08.448 --> 00:32:09.588
motion inside the vessel.

00:32:10.648 --> 00:32:14.208
The isolated whole has positive entropy generation while its surroundings

00:32:14.208 --> 00:32:15.168
exchange no heat.

00:32:15.988 --> 00:32:20.028
A real gas can show a temperature change in an analogous expansion, and the

00:32:20.028 --> 00:32:23.928
reversible adiabatic volume temperature power law cannot be imposed on this

00:32:23.928 --> 00:32:24.788
irreversible event.

00:32:25.128 --> 00:32:29.825
When an ideal gas changes both temperature and volume, keeping just one

00:32:29.825 --> 00:32:30.288
entropy

00:32:30.288 --> 00:32:32.488
term misses part of the endpoint change.

00:32:33.528 --> 00:32:37.228
Construct a reversible reference path consisting of constant volume warming

00:32:37.228 --> 00:32:40.468
followed by isothermal expansion at the final temperature.

00:32:41.288 --> 00:32:46.728
For constant molar heat capacity, the warming contribution is n C V times the

00:32:46.728 --> 00:32:48.828
natural logarithm of final over initial temperature.

00:32:49.528 --> 00:32:54.308
The expansion contribution is n R times the logarithm of final over initial

00:32:54.308 --> 00:32:54.708
volume.

00:32:55.128 --> 00:32:57.988
Add them because entropy is a state function.

00:32:58.888 --> 00:33:03.428
Take one mole of a monatomic ideal gas with C V equal to three halves R,

00:33:04.028 --> 00:33:08.278
temperature increasing from three hundred to six hundred kelvin and volume

00:33:08.278 --> 00:33:08.508
from

00:33:08.508 --> 00:33:10.788
ten to twenty litres.

00:33:10.788 --> 00:33:15.508
Each logarithm is the natural logarithm of two, so the sum is five halves R

00:33:15.508 --> 00:33:19.688
times that logarithm, approximately fourteen point four one joules per kelvin.

00:33:21.208 --> 00:33:23.108
Reversing both endpoints changes the sign.

00:33:23.608 --> 00:33:27.688
These endpoints even have the same ideal gas pressure because temperature and

00:33:27.688 --> 00:33:31.528
volume double together, but that does not make the entropy change zero.

00:33:32.368 --> 00:33:36.673
The actual process may follow a different path; its heat and work are not

00:33:36.673 --> 00:33:36.968
fixed

00:33:36.968 --> 00:33:38.628
by this entropy calculation alone.

00:33:38.736 --> 00:33:42.716
An equilibrium constant belongs to a specified temperature as well as a

00:33:42.716 --> 00:33:43.976
specified balanced reaction.

00:33:44.676 --> 00:33:48.076
A supporting integrated van't Hoff calculation predicts its temperature change

00:33:48.076 --> 00:33:51.396
when reaction enthalpy is approximately constant over the interval.

00:33:52.196 --> 00:33:57.176
The natural logarithm of K two divided by K one equals minus standard reaction

00:33:57.176 --> 00:34:02.416
enthalpy divided by R, multiplied by one over temperature two minus one over

00:34:02.416 --> 00:34:03.636
temperature one.

00:34:03.636 --> 00:34:07.196
Suppose the supplied reaction has enthalpy plus thirty kilojoules per mole,

00:34:07.736 --> 00:34:10.236
with K equal to two at three hundred kelvin.

00:34:11.016 --> 00:34:14.966
At three hundred thirty kelvin, first convert the enthalpy to thirty thousand

00:34:14.966 --> 00:34:19.116
joules per mole to match R eight point three one four.

00:34:19.116 --> 00:34:23.399
The logarithm of the constant ratio is approximately one point zero nine

00:34:23.399 --> 00:34:23.756
three,

00:34:23.756 --> 00:34:27.936
giving a ratio about two point nine eight five and a new constant about five

00:34:27.936 --> 00:34:29.096
point nine seven.

00:34:29.096 --> 00:34:31.476
The increase agrees with the endothermic sign.

00:34:31.936 --> 00:34:35.656
For a negative reaction enthalpy over the same heating interval, the constant

00:34:35.656 --> 00:34:36.356
would decrease.

00:34:37.636 --> 00:34:41.296
Do not replace the natural logarithm with a base ten logarithm without its

00:34:41.296 --> 00:34:45.676
conversion factor, or hold enthalpy constant across a phase change without

00:34:45.676 --> 00:34:46.376
checking the model.

00:34:46.584 --> 00:34:51.604
Neutralization data can reveal a hidden ionization step if all reactions and

00:34:51.604 --> 00:34:52.324
states are matched.

00:34:52.964 --> 00:34:58.184
In a supplied dilute solution Hess model, hydrogen ion plus hydroxide forms

00:34:58.184 --> 00:35:01.864
water with enthalpy minus fifty seven kilojoules per mole water.

00:35:02.884 --> 00:35:08.064
A weak acid H A reacting with hydroxide gives A minus and water with enthalpy

00:35:08.064 --> 00:35:10.544
minus fifty five kilojoules per mole water.

00:35:11.444 --> 00:35:16.264
Add the formal ionization of H A into hydrogen ion and A minus to the first

00:35:16.264 --> 00:35:16.684
reaction.

00:35:17.724 --> 00:35:21.364
Hydrogen ions cancel, leaving the weak acid neutralization equation.

00:35:22.264 --> 00:35:26.964
Therefore ionization enthalpy plus minus fifty seven equals minus fifty five,

00:35:26.964 --> 00:35:29.864
and ionization enthalpy is plus two kilojoules per mole.

00:35:30.684 --> 00:35:34.864
In this dataset the endothermic ionization step offsets some heat released by

00:35:34.864 --> 00:35:35.464
water formation.

00:35:36.244 --> 00:35:40.364
This is not a universal numerical value for every weak acid, and weak

00:35:40.364 --> 00:35:44.004
ionization is not automatically an endothermic process in every case.

00:35:45.004 --> 00:35:48.484
Compare like amounts of water and compatible dilute solution states before

00:35:48.484 --> 00:35:49.464
subtracting measurements.

00:35:50.624 --> 00:35:54.584
Heat absorbed by a calorimeter has the opposite sign to the reaction heat, so

00:35:54.584 --> 00:35:58.324
an observed temperature rise must first be converted to a consistently signed

00:35:58.324 --> 00:35:59.084
reaction enthalpy.

00:35:59.832 --> 00:36:04.212
Two isomers can have the same molecular formula but different enthalpies.

00:36:04.952 --> 00:36:08.648
A combustion cycle can measure their difference if the product amounts,

00:36:08.648 --> 00:36:09.012
product

00:36:09.012 --> 00:36:10.712
phases and temperature match.

00:36:11.732 --> 00:36:15.702
Use supplied illustrative molar combustion enthalpies minus three thousand

00:36:15.702 --> 00:36:19.912
kilojoules for isomer A and minus two thousand nine hundred eighty for isomer

00:36:19.912 --> 00:36:24.112
B, each leading to the same carbon dioxide and liquid water endpoint.

00:36:24.832 --> 00:36:27.912
Burn A, then reverse the combustion equation for B.

00:36:28.892 --> 00:36:32.792
Oxygen and all combustion products cancel, leaving A converting into B.

00:36:33.572 --> 00:36:37.722
The enthalpy change is minus three thousand minus minus two thousand nine

00:36:37.722 --> 00:36:40.172
hundred eighty, giving minus twenty kilojoules per mole.

00:36:41.072 --> 00:36:45.972
B is therefore lower in enthalpy by twenty kilojoules per mole for the stated

00:36:45.972 --> 00:36:47.072
phases and conditions.

00:36:47.912 --> 00:36:52.812
Do not reverse this conclusion because A releases more heat on combustion: its

00:36:52.812 --> 00:36:55.272
larger release starts from the higher enthalpy state.

00:36:56.372 --> 00:37:00.112
Enthalpy ordering alone does not establish a Gibbs energy ordering, an

00:37:00.112 --> 00:37:02.592
equilibrium composition or a reaction rate.

00:37:03.432 --> 00:37:07.072
Entropy, temperature and activation barriers are separate information.

00:37:07.652 --> 00:37:11.232
If the combustion data use different water phases or different temperatures,

00:37:11.992 --> 00:37:14.092
correct those endpoints before subtracting the numbers.

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:18.960
A combustion enthalpy is incomplete unless the product states are specified.

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:23.380
Producing water vapor leaves the products at a higher enthalpy than producing

00:37:23.380 --> 00:37:24.960
liquid water at the same temperature.

00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:30.080
In a supplied methane example, take the combustion enthalpy to carbon dioxide

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:35.080
gas and two liquid water molecules as minus eight hundred ninety kilojoules

00:37:35.080 --> 00:37:35.280
per

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:35.920
mole of methane.

00:37:36.920 --> 00:37:41.100
Use an illustrative water vaporization enthalpy of forty four kilojoules per

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:42.480
mole at that same temperature.

00:37:42.990 --> 00:37:47.600
To change the endpoint to two water vapor molecules, add two vaporization

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:50.400
steps, a total of plus eighty eight kilojoules.

00:37:51.200 --> 00:37:55.280
The gas water combustion enthalpy is therefore minus eight hundred two

00:37:55.280 --> 00:37:56.140
kilojoules per mole.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:01.327
It remains exothermic but releases less heat in magnitude because the water

00:38:01.327 --> 00:38:01.500
has

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:02.100
not condensed.

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:06.400
Reversing the endpoint change requires subtracting those vaporization

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:06.960
contributions.

00:38:07.600 --> 00:38:12.062
The coefficient is the number of water moles produced per written reaction,

00:38:12.062 --> 00:38:12.240
not

00:38:12.240 --> 00:38:13.480
the number of hydrogen atoms.

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:18.171
These are matched temperature enthalpy corrections, not an instruction to use

00:38:18.171 --> 00:38:18.220
a

00:38:18.220 --> 00:38:21.980
boiling point latent heat at an unrelated temperature without adjustment.

00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:26.180
Always attach the phase labels before comparing fuel energy data.

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:31.560
Gibbs energy connects a process to a limit on useful work when temperature and

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:32.520
pressure are held fixed.

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:36.272
Use the chemistry sign convention in which work done on the system is

00:38:36.272 --> 00:38:36.660
positive.

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:41.400
For a reversible process between specified states at fixed temperature and

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:45.100
pressure, the nonexpansion work done on the system equals its Gibbs energy

00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:45.500
change.

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:50.580
If that change is minus forty kilojoules, the maximum useful work delivered to

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:54.000
the surroundings has positive magnitude forty kilojoules.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:57.740
The work on the system is negative forty; these are two descriptions with

00:38:57.740 --> 00:39:00.680
opposite sign conventions, not contradictory answers.

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:05.240
Pressure volume displacement work is excluded from this useful nonexpansion

00:39:05.240 --> 00:39:05.740
work limit.

00:39:06.660 --> 00:39:10.800
Actual irreversible operation delivers less useful work for the same specified

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:11.840
overall state change.

00:39:12.900 --> 00:39:16.980
A device delivering twenty five kilojoules in this example falls below the

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:21.326
forty kilojoule limit; it does not violate energy conservation because heat

00:39:21.326 --> 00:39:21.500
and

00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:23.940
other energy transfers also enter the full balance.

00:39:24.680 --> 00:39:29.080
Do not substitute enthalpy for Gibbs energy or use a standard reaction value

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:31.780
for an arbitrary nonstandard finite transformation.

00:39:33.020 --> 00:39:36.540
Identify the actual states, the environmental constraints and whether the

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:39.260
requested answer is work on the system or work delivered.

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:43.560
A reaction enthalpy temperature correction must follow the actual phases.

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:48.101
Consider an illustrative one to one conversion of A into B at a fixed

00:39:48.101 --> 00:39:48.560
pressure.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:53.415
At three hundred kelvin both substances are solids and the reaction enthalpy

00:39:53.415 --> 00:39:53.520
is

00:39:53.520 --> 00:39:54.840
twenty kilojoules per mole.

00:39:55.800 --> 00:40:00.140
A melts at three hundred fifty kelvin with fusion enthalpy five kilojoules per

00:40:00.140 --> 00:40:03.080
mole, while B remains solid to four hundred kelvin.

00:40:03.820 --> 00:40:08.880
Use constant molar heat capacities thirty for solid A, fifty for liquid A and

00:40:08.880 --> 00:40:11.720
forty for solid B, all in joules per mole kelvin.

00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:17.080
From three hundred to three hundred fifty, products minus reactants gives plus

00:40:17.080 --> 00:40:21.500
ten joules per mole kelvin, raising the reaction enthalpy by half a kilojoule.

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:26.200
Melting raises the reactant enthalpy by five kilojoules, so the reaction

00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:28.440
enthalpy drops by five, not rises.

00:40:29.120 --> 00:40:33.234
Above the transition, the heat capacity difference is minus ten, giving

00:40:33.234 --> 00:40:33.640
another

00:40:33.640 --> 00:40:34.720
negative half kilojoule.

00:40:35.480 --> 00:40:39.580
The final reaction enthalpy for liquid A becoming solid B at four hundred

00:40:39.580 --> 00:40:41.820
kelvin is fifteen kilojoules per mole.

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:46.440
A single heat capacity integral that omits fusion cannot connect these

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:46.820
endpoints.

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:51.320
This supporting piecewise model assumes no other transition or temperature

00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:51.800
dependence.

00:40:52.104 --> 00:40:56.544
Returning to the same thermodynamic state does not mean that no energy was

00:40:56.544 --> 00:40:57.564
transferred along the way.

00:40:58.444 --> 00:41:02.244
Consider a closed working substance following a specified quasistatic

00:41:02.244 --> 00:41:04.104
rectangular pressure volume cycle.

00:41:05.004 --> 00:41:09.544
It expands from one to three litres at two bar, changes pressure at fixed

00:41:09.544 --> 00:41:14.484
volume, compresses back at one bar, and returns to its original pressure at

00:41:14.484 --> 00:41:15.024
fixed volume.

00:41:15.584 --> 00:41:20.164
The net work done by the substance is the enclosed area: one bar times two

00:41:20.164 --> 00:41:21.884
litres, or two hundred joules.

00:41:22.624 --> 00:41:26.664
In the chemistry convention, work on the system is therefore minus two hundred

00:41:26.664 --> 00:41:27.024
joules.

00:41:27.984 --> 00:41:32.164
Because internal energy returns to its initial value, the first law requires

00:41:32.164 --> 00:41:34.944
net heat into the system of plus two hundred joules.

00:41:35.824 --> 00:41:40.184
The system entropy also returns to its original value, but that alone does not

00:41:40.184 --> 00:41:42.484
prove zero entropy generation in the surroundings.

00:41:43.084 --> 00:41:47.704
Heat and work are path transfers; internal energy and entropy are state

00:41:47.704 --> 00:41:48.144
functions.

00:41:49.104 --> 00:41:53.144
The loop area gives net work, not the total heat absorbed on only the hot

00:41:53.144 --> 00:41:55.944
portions or an efficiency without additional information.

00:41:57.360 --> 00:42:00.980
The surroundings in a heat transfer problem are not always an infinite

00:42:00.980 --> 00:42:02.440
reservoir at fixed temperature.

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:06.980
Take two bodies with equal constant heat capacities of one hundred joules per

00:42:06.980 --> 00:42:10.220
kelvin, initially at four hundred and three hundred kelvin.

00:42:11.120 --> 00:42:15.120
Place the pair in an ideal insulated enclosure, allow only heat exchange

00:42:15.120 --> 00:42:18.420
between them, and neglect mechanical work and phase changes.

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:22.660
Energy conservation gives a common final temperature of three hundred fifty

00:42:22.660 --> 00:42:23.000
kelvin.

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:27.680
The hotter body loses five thousand joules and the cooler gains the same

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:30.420
amount, so the combined energy change is zero.

00:42:31.300 --> 00:42:32.360
Entropy is a separate ledger.

00:42:33.280 --> 00:42:36.885
Evaluate each state change along a reversible reference heating or cooling

00:42:36.885 --> 00:42:37.080
path

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:41.854
using heat capacity times the natural logarithm of final temperature divided

00:42:41.854 --> 00:42:41.980
by

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:42.660
initial temperature.

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:46.940
Adding the two changes gives one hundred times the logarithm of forty nine

00:42:46.940 --> 00:42:50.980
divided by forty eight, about two point zero six joules per kelvin.

00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:55.060
The total is positive even though no heat crossed the enclosure boundary.

00:42:55.820 --> 00:42:59.880
This models spontaneous finite temperature heat exchange; replacing both

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:04.680
logarithms by one heat divided by an arbitrary average temperature would lose

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:06.200
the entropy generation information.

00:43:06.408 --> 00:43:11.688
A calorimeter temperature rise becomes a reaction energy only after the

00:43:11.688 --> 00:43:13.268
effective heat capacity is known.

00:43:13.988 --> 00:43:18.788
In a selected calibration, an electrical heater supplies two hundred watts for

00:43:18.788 --> 00:43:22.628
one hundred seconds, depositing twenty kilojoules while the measured

00:43:22.628 --> 00:43:24.588
temperature rises four kelvin.

00:43:25.508 --> 00:43:29.828
Assume heat leakage and other losses have been corrected or are negligible.

00:43:30.268 --> 00:43:34.768
The effective heat capacity of the complete calorimeter assembly is then five

00:43:34.768 --> 00:43:35.788
kilojoules per kelvin.

00:43:36.708 --> 00:43:41.073
Under the same assembly conditions, a reaction produces a three kelvin rise,

00:43:41.073 --> 00:43:41.188
so

00:43:41.188 --> 00:43:43.368
the calorimeter gains fifteen kilojoules.

00:43:44.008 --> 00:43:46.728
The reacting sample loses that energy as heat.

00:43:47.468 --> 00:43:51.288
If the sample corresponds to zero point zero one zero mole of the written

00:43:51.288 --> 00:43:55.473
reaction, its molar energy change is minus one thousand five hundred

00:43:55.473 --> 00:43:56.088
kilojoules

00:43:56.088 --> 00:43:56.568
per mole.

00:43:57.068 --> 00:44:01.108
In the stipulated rigid constant volume experiment with no other significant

00:44:01.108 --> 00:44:04.388
work, this measures a reaction internal energy change.

00:44:05.048 --> 00:44:07.388
It is not automatically a constant pressure enthalpy.

00:44:08.468 --> 00:44:12.708
Ignition energy, evaporation, changed filling and background heat can require

00:44:12.708 --> 00:44:16.968
separate corrections in a real instrument; calibration is not permission to

00:44:16.968 --> 00:44:17.768
ignore those differences.

00:44:18.168 --> 00:44:22.688
Using an enthalpy from one temperature with an entropy from another can create

00:44:22.688 --> 00:44:24.528
an inconsistent Gibbs calculation.

00:44:25.328 --> 00:44:29.349
In a supporting constant heat capacity model, take a standard reaction

00:44:29.349 --> 00:44:29.808
enthalpy

00:44:29.808 --> 00:44:34.788
of forty kilojoules per mole and entropy of one hundred joules per mole per

00:44:34.788 --> 00:44:36.068
kelvin at three hundred kelvin.

00:44:37.048 --> 00:44:41.208
Let the reaction heat capacity difference be fifty joules per mole per kelvin

00:44:41.208 --> 00:44:45.208
throughout the interval to six hundred kelvin, with no phase transition.

00:44:45.778 --> 00:44:49.988
The enthalpy correction is the heat capacity difference times the temperature

00:44:49.988 --> 00:44:52.708
interval, adding fifteen kilojoules per mole.

00:44:53.468 --> 00:44:57.488
The entropy correction instead integrates heat capacity difference divided by

00:44:57.488 --> 00:45:01.928
temperature, adding fifty times the natural logarithm of two joules per mole

00:45:01.928 --> 00:45:02.468
per kelvin.

00:45:03.208 --> 00:45:07.568
At six hundred kelvin, the consistent enthalpy is fifty five kilojoules per

00:45:07.568 --> 00:45:11.848
mole and entropy about one hundred thirty four point six six joules per mole

00:45:11.848 --> 00:45:12.468
per kelvin.

00:45:13.148 --> 00:45:17.808
Subtracting temperature times entropy gives standard Gibbs energy about minus

00:45:17.808 --> 00:45:20.928
twenty five point seven nine kilojoules per mole.

00:45:21.668 --> 00:45:25.568
Use compatible units and the same reaction with the stated constant heat

00:45:25.568 --> 00:45:26.348
capacity difference.

00:45:26.616 --> 00:45:31.216
The entropy change of fusion is evaluated at phase coexistence, but the free

00:45:31.216 --> 00:45:34.256
energy difference can also compare the phases at nearby temperatures.

00:45:35.156 --> 00:45:38.442
Use a supplied pure substance whose melting temperature is three hundred

00:45:38.442 --> 00:45:38.716
kelvin

00:45:38.716 --> 00:45:43.076
at the stated pressure and whose fusion enthalpy is six thousand joules per

00:45:43.076 --> 00:45:43.196
mole.

00:45:44.016 --> 00:45:48.240
At coexistence the fusion entropy is twenty joules per mole per kelvin,

00:45:48.240 --> 00:45:48.656
because

00:45:48.656 --> 00:45:50.276
the fusion free energy is zero.

00:45:51.176 --> 00:45:54.758
Now assume the fusion enthalpy and entropy remain approximately constant

00:45:54.758 --> 00:45:55.056
across

00:45:55.056 --> 00:45:56.216
a narrow temperature range.

00:45:57.056 --> 00:46:01.216
At two hundred ninety kelvin, enthalpy minus temperature times entropy gives

00:46:01.216 --> 00:46:03.816
fusion free energy plus two hundred joules per mole.

00:46:04.916 --> 00:46:08.216
Melting is then unfavourable in this comparison, while the reverse freezing

00:46:08.216 --> 00:46:09.756
change has negative free energy.

00:46:10.796 --> 00:46:14.796
At three hundred ten kelvin the fusion free energy is minus two hundred joules

00:46:14.796 --> 00:46:17.496
per mole, favouring liquid relative to solid.

00:46:18.156 --> 00:46:20.456
At three hundred kelvin the difference is zero.

00:46:21.196 --> 00:46:25.503
A positive fusion enthalpy by itself does not decide which phase is favoured

00:46:25.503 --> 00:46:25.616
at

00:46:25.616 --> 00:46:26.296
every temperature.

00:46:27.376 --> 00:46:30.809
This simple model neglects heat capacity differences between phases over the

00:46:30.809 --> 00:46:31.216
interval.

00:46:31.976 --> 00:46:36.236
It also does not predict how quickly crystallization begins; a supercooled

00:46:36.236 --> 00:46:40.056
liquid can persist because nucleation and kinetics are separate from the

00:46:40.056 --> 00:46:41.536
equilibrium free energy comparison.

00:46:41.784 --> 00:46:46.944
Adiabatic means no heat crosses the chosen boundary; it does not guarantee

00:46:46.944 --> 00:46:47.904
constant entropy.

00:46:48.964 --> 00:46:53.224
Consider a stipulated closed rigid insulated vessel containing one mole of

00:46:53.224 --> 00:46:57.562
ideal gas with constant molar heat capacity at constant volume of twenty

00:46:57.562 --> 00:46:57.924
joules

00:46:57.924 --> 00:46:58.944
per mole per kelvin.

00:46:59.784 --> 00:47:02.044
Let the initial temperature be three hundred kelvin.

00:47:02.824 --> 00:47:06.433
A stirrer supplies one thousand joules of shaft work that ultimately

00:47:06.433 --> 00:47:06.964
dissipates

00:47:06.964 --> 00:47:07.764
into the gas.

00:47:08.864 --> 00:47:13.184
Neglect energy stored in the vessel or stirrer and assume the final gas is

00:47:13.184 --> 00:47:14.104
again at equilibrium.

00:47:14.944 --> 00:47:19.564
There is no volume work because the vessel is rigid, but the shaft work still

00:47:19.564 --> 00:47:20.184
crosses the boundary.

00:47:20.944 --> 00:47:24.704
With the Chemistry convention that work on the system is positive, internal

00:47:24.704 --> 00:47:28.584
energy increases by one thousand joules while heat is zero.

00:47:29.624 --> 00:47:32.804
Dividing by the total heat capacity gives a temperature rise of fifty kelvin,

00:47:33.244 --> 00:47:36.024
so the final temperature is three hundred fifty.

00:47:36.024 --> 00:47:40.424
The entropy change follows from the equilibrium endpoints at fixed volume: one

00:47:40.424 --> 00:47:44.360
mole times twenty times the natural logarithm of three hundred fifty divided

00:47:44.360 --> 00:47:44.464
by

00:47:44.464 --> 00:47:48.364
three hundred, about three point zero eight three joules per kelvin.

00:47:49.204 --> 00:47:53.044
Since no entropy enters with heat in this model, that positive change is

00:47:53.044 --> 00:47:54.304
entropy generated internally.

00:47:55.284 --> 00:47:58.144
Work transfers energy without carrying the heat entropy term.

00:47:59.004 --> 00:48:01.224
Adiabatic and reversible are distinct conditions.

00:48:01.896 --> 00:48:05.975
The temperature dependence of equilibrium vapour pressure can be estimated

00:48:05.975 --> 00:48:06.196
with

00:48:06.196 --> 00:48:08.916
a stated form of the Clausius Clapeyron relation.

00:48:09.836 --> 00:48:13.806
Use a hypothetical pure liquid with vapour pressure ten kilopascals at three

00:48:13.806 --> 00:48:18.656
hundred kelvin and supplied vaporization enthalpy forty kilojoules per mole.

00:48:19.256 --> 00:48:23.676
Treat that enthalpy as constant over the interval, the vapour as ideal and the

00:48:23.676 --> 00:48:27.236
liquid molar volume as negligible compared with the vapour.

00:48:27.236 --> 00:48:31.256
The natural logarithm of the pressure ratio equals vaporization enthalpy

00:48:31.256 --> 00:48:36.104
divided by the gas constant, multiplied by reciprocal initial temperature

00:48:36.104 --> 00:48:36.436
minus

00:48:36.436 --> 00:48:37.796
reciprocal final temperature.

00:48:39.276 --> 00:48:42.991
Convert forty kilojoules to forty thousand joules before using the gas

00:48:42.991 --> 00:48:43.416
constant

00:48:43.416 --> 00:48:46.236
eight point three one four joules per mole per kelvin.

00:48:47.656 --> 00:48:51.456
For a final temperature of three hundred thirty kelvin, the logarithmic ratio

00:48:51.456 --> 00:48:56.071
is about one point four five eight, giving a pressure ratio about four point

00:48:56.071 --> 00:48:57.796
two nine seven.

00:48:57.796 --> 00:49:01.556
The predicted vapour pressure is therefore about forty two point nine seven

00:49:01.556 --> 00:49:02.236
kilopascals.

00:49:03.376 --> 00:49:06.916
Both pressure values must use the same unit inside their ratio, and

00:49:06.916 --> 00:49:08.616
temperatures must be absolute kelvin.

00:49:09.476 --> 00:49:12.796
A vapour pressure below an external pressure of one hundred one point three

00:49:12.796 --> 00:49:17.256
kilopascals does not yet satisfy the ordinary bulk boiling criterion at that

00:49:17.256 --> 00:49:17.856
external pressure.

00:49:17.856 --> 00:49:22.416
The model estimates equilibrium pressure, not evaporation rate.

00:49:22.656 --> 00:49:26.056
Pressure does not shift every melting temperature in the same direction.

00:49:27.076 --> 00:49:31.336
Along a pure substance solid liquid coexistence line, the Clapeyron relation

00:49:31.336 --> 00:49:34.836
links the slope to the entropy and volume changes of fusion.

00:49:35.636 --> 00:49:39.796
In inverse form, the melting temperature slope with pressure equals melting

00:49:39.796 --> 00:49:43.706
temperature times the molar volume change divided by the molar fusion

00:49:43.706 --> 00:49:44.216
enthalpy.

00:49:45.476 --> 00:49:50.176
Use a supplied local model at three hundred kelvin, with fusion enthalpy six

00:49:50.176 --> 00:49:54.556
thousand joules per mole and fusion volume change minus one cubic centimetre

00:49:54.556 --> 00:49:55.016
per mole.

00:49:56.556 --> 00:50:00.403
Convert that volume to minus one times ten to the minus six cubic metres per

00:50:00.403 --> 00:50:00.656
mole.

00:50:01.656 --> 00:50:05.576
The temperature slope is then minus five times ten to the minus eight kelvin

00:50:05.576 --> 00:50:10.236
per pascal, or minus zero point zero five zero kelvin per megapascal.

00:50:11.376 --> 00:50:14.709
Increasing pressure by ten megapascals gives an approximate melting

00:50:14.709 --> 00:50:15.256
temperature

00:50:15.256 --> 00:50:19.576
decrease of zero point five zero kelvin under this local constant slope model.

00:50:20.456 --> 00:50:24.656
The negative slope occurs because the liquid has smaller molar volume than the

00:50:24.656 --> 00:50:25.416
solid in this example.

00:50:26.556 --> 00:50:30.436
With positive fusion volume change and positive fusion enthalpy, the slope

00:50:30.436 --> 00:50:31.616
would instead be positive.

00:50:32.656 --> 00:50:36.616
These are hypothetical supplied values, not a precision prediction for water

00:50:36.616 --> 00:50:37.936
over a large pressure range.

00:50:38.796 --> 00:50:42.976
The relation concerns the equilibrium coexistence curve, not the rate of

00:50:42.976 --> 00:50:43.456
melting or freezing.
